  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "note_53",
      "text": "Finds when a rule over one or more series first (and next) becomes sustainedly true. It is the detection step for both set-point alerts (e.g. temperature below 33 for 6 h) and baseline-relative monitoring (a rolling rhythm metric outside its baseline mean ± 3 SD). Build the rule from conditions (series above/below a typed value or a wired scalar threshold) combined as OR of AND-groups; a condition on x acts as a gate. Persistence requires the rule to hold continuously (in x units when x is wired, hours when x is a time column, else consecutive values) before a crossing fires, and the rule must go false again before the next one can fire, so one long excursion is one crossing. Outputs: every crossing's x value (or index), their count, and the 0/1 breach series; missing data, or a gap in x of more than 1.5 sampling intervals (rows absent from the file), breaks a persistence run rather than completing it. In the demo, read the crossings as the moments the activity rhythm dips below the cutoff for the required duration: one per trough, re-armed in between. Statistical-process-control style rule detection; see also Threshold for the simple per-sample 0/1 case.",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 24,
      "width": 360,
      "height": 190
    }
